module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Intersection.Projections.DualClosedPoints
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTwistChain.MY
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Linear subspaces of projective space and the projections away from them

A submodule `W` of a vector space `V` cuts out a projective linear subspace of
`P(V) = Proj (Sym_k V)`, namely the projective zero locus of the degree-one forms coming from
`W`. Dually, a point of `P(V)` has a space of degree-one forms vanishing at it; for a closed
point over an algebraically closed field this is a hyperplane `W_p` of `V` whose linear subspace
is the point itself, and the intersection of the hyperplanes of two distinct closed points has
corank two, so it cuts out the line joining them.

The inclusion of a submodule induces a graded homomorphism of symmetric algebras, and the
morphism of projective spectra it induces is defined exactly on the complement of the linear
subspace cut out by that submodule: this is the linear projection `P(V) ⊃ P(V) ∖ L(W) → P(W)`,
specializing for `W = W_p` to the projection `r_p` away from a closed point `p`.

The dimension of `L(W)` is the subject of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Variety.Projective.LinearSubspaceDimension`, which is a separate
file because the dimension of a projective space is proved from a large part of the library.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.pointSubmodule`: the degree-one forms vanishing at
  a point of `P(V)`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.linearSubspace`: the projective linear subspace cut
  out by a submodule of `V`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.projection`: the linear projection away from that
  subspace.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.lineSubmodule`: the degree-one forms vanishing at
  two points, whose linear subspace is the line joining them.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.linearSubspace_ker` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.linearSubspace_pointSubmodule`: the hyperplane of
  a closed point cuts out exactly that point.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.notMem_mapDomain_iff_mem_linearSubspace` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.coe_mapDomain_subtype`: the domain of definition of
  the projection away from `W` is the complement of the linear subspace cut out by `W`; for a
  closed point this is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.coe_mapDomain_pointSubmodule`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.mem_projection_iff` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.projection_eq_linearFunctionalPoint`: the
  projection on points.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.mem_linearSubspace_inf_of_projection_eq`: two
  points with the same image under the projection away from `W` lie on a common linear subspace
  cut out by `W` together with the second point; for `W = W_p` this is the line joining them.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.finrank_quotient_lineSubmodule`: the forms
  vanishing at two distinct closed points have corank two, so they cut out a line, and
  `left_mem_linearSubspace_lineSubmodule`, `right_mem_linearSubspace_lineSubmodule` say that it
  passes through both.

## Implementation notes

The projection is `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTwistChain.mapHom` of the graded homomorphism
`SymPow.gradedSymAlgMap W.subtype`, so it is defined on
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjShared.mapDomain` of that homomorphism rather than on all of
`P(V)`; identifying that open as the complement of `linearSubspace` is what makes it usable.

A closed point is handled through its linear functional: over an algebraically closed field with
`V` finite-dimensional every closed point is
`AlgebraicGeometry.ProjectiveSpaceOfModule.linearFunctionalPoint` of a nonzero functional, and
the identification of the hyperplane with that functional's kernel rests on the presentation of
the kernel of `Sym f` as the ideal generated by `ker f`.

